schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: 2-of-3 multisig is vulnerable; 3-of-5 recommended for community treasuries
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "Threshold set too low" is named as the first common mistake: a 2-of-3
    multisig is vulnerable because if any two signers collude or become
    unavailable, funds are at risk or inaccessible; the article recommends
    3-of-5 or higher for community treasuries, and states "the threshold
    should be more than half the signers, but less than all of them."
  evidence: >-
    Presented as the article's own security reasoning/best-practice
    guidance, not attributed to an external audit, incident dataset, or
    named case of an actual 2-of-3 multisig failure.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/multisig-setup.md
  interpretation: >-
    Sound, widely-accepted operational-security logic (more signers reduces
    single/dual-point-of-failure risk) but stated as general best practice
    rather than backed by a cited incident study; the specific numeric
    thresholds (2-of-3 vs 3-of-5 vs 4-of-7) are heuristics, not derived
    from a named risk model.
  uncertainty: >-
    Low for the qualitative principle (higher, majority-but-not-unanimous
    thresholds reduce risk — well-established in multisig/threshold-
    cryptography practice); moderate for the specific "3-of-5 is the
    sensible default" recommendation, which is asserted rather than
    evidenced against community-size or treasury-size data.
  high_risk: true
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/multisig-setup.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
